%description:

Protocol Test Framework self test -- a cardinality at a receiving module.

Every other count self test measures at the sender. This one measures at the receiver,
because the two sides used to disagree. `host1` sends five datagrams and `host2` receives
five, so `exactlyTimes(5, ...)` on `host2.udp` must hold.

It did not. `Udp` raised `packetReceivedFromLower` twice for one datagram: its base class
`LayeredProtocolBase::handleLowerMessage` emitted the signal and then called
`handleLowerPacket`, which reached `processUDPPacket`, which emitted it again. Five
datagrams counted as ten, so this check failed with "more than 5 occurrence(s)" and
`exactlyTimes(10, ...)` passed. No statistic doubled, because nothing in `Udp.ned` sources
that signal, so only a listener saw it. The duplicate emit is gone from `Udp.cc`, and this
test is what holds it gone.

A count is the only step that sees the difference. A `never` fails on one match or on two,
and an open-world `once` resolves on the first and ignores the rest, which is why the whole
suite passed over the fault.

Expected result: PASS.

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("count_at_receiver")
        .exactlyTimes(5, on("host2.udp").signal("packetReceivedFromLower")
                  .filterPacket("udp.destPort == 5000")
                  .describe("a datagram arriving at host2's UDP")
                  .within(0.95));
}

%activity:
// present only so opp_test compiles the %global above; this module is not in the network.
EV << "count_at_receiver compiled\n";

%file: test.ned

import inet.node.inet.StandardHost;
import inet.node.ethernet.Eth100M;
import inet.networklayer.configurator.ipv4.Ipv4NetworkConfigurator;

// Two hosts on one link -- note: NO tester (attached at runtime).
network SelfTestNet
{
    submodules:
        configurator: Ipv4NetworkConfigurator { @display("p=100,50"); }
        host1: StandardHost { @display("p=300,100"); }
        host2: StandardHost { @display("p=500,100"); }
    connections:
        host1.ethg++ <--> Eth100M <--> host2.ethg++;
}

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
network = SelfTestNet
sim-time-limit = 1s
seed-set = 0

**.ipv4.arp.typename = "GlobalArp"

# Five datagrams: 0.1s, 0.3s, 0.5s, 0.7s, 0.9s.
*.host1.numApps = 1
*.host1.app[0].typename = "UdpBasicApp"
*.host1.app[0].destAddresses = "host2"
*.host1.app[0].destPort = 5000
*.host1.app[0].localPort = 4000
*.host1.app[0].messageLength = 100B
*.host1.app[0].sendInterval = 0.2s
*.host1.app[0].startTime = 0.1s
*.host2.numApps = 1
*.host2.app[0].typename = "UdpSink"
*.host2.app[0].localPort = 5000

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST count_at_receiver: PASS
